1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,672
What stops two clicks on the pay button from charging the card twice?

2
00:00:03,722 --> 00:00:09,240
Two requests to confirm the same payment can arrive at the same moment, and both would try to debit.

3
00:00:09,290 --> 00:00:15,048
One conditional update moves the payment from confirmed to debiting, and only one caller can win it.

4
00:00:15,098 --> 00:00:18,960
Anyone who loses the claim simply gets the payment as it stands.

5
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:26,472
The winner fixes the route: the website's live e POS terminal for the card's scheme, with its MID and TID.

6
00:00:26,522 --> 00:00:33,024
It fixes the fee too: the most specific commission rule, the fee and the merchant's net, kept on the payment.

7
00:00:33,074 --> 00:00:39,528
With no e POS terminal or no fee rule, the payment fails right there, before any money moves.

8
00:00:39,578 --> 00:00:47,496
Every call to the scheme is written to an operation journal first, and the database allows only one debit per payment.

9
00:00:47,546 --> 00:00:55,752
Then the card module asks the scheme through its adapter, with our payment id as the reference, so a retry is never paid twice.

10
00:00:55,802 --> 00:01:00,528
An approval makes the payment paid, with the scheme's reference number, the RRN.

11
00:01:00,578 --> 00:01:03,528
A decline is final, with our own code for why.

12
00:01:03,578 --> 00:01:10,272
No answer within two seconds leaves it unknown, never declined, because the money may already have moved.

13
00:01:10,322 --> 00:01:20,208
Every move goes through the status table, so a paid payment never becomes unpaid; it only moves on when its money goes back, which episode twenty-one explains.

14
00:01:20,258 --> 00:01:30,072
The page asks for the result every second for five seconds, then tells the buyer not to pay again; the bank sees the fee, the e POS terminal and the RRN.

15
00:01:30,122 --> 00:01:33,456
Next, seeing inside the system when something is slow.
